VCOS
====

VCOS is a small, modular virtual-console operating system. It boots as a
minimal Linux/Buildroot image, presents a native runtime, and loads each
emulator or system implementation as a versioned native module instead of
rebuilding the whole OS.

The project is currently a QEMU x86_64 development image. It is not yet a
general-purpose console release.


IDEA
----

A console OS should separate three things:

1. The platform
   Boot, display, audio, input, storage, module lifecycle and updates.

2. The systems
   NES, SNES, Genesis, PlayStation, Nintendo 64, and so on.

3. The content
   ROMs, disc images, BIOSes and save data.

VCOS owns the platform. A module owns one system. A .vmod package carries a
module and its manifest. The platform verifies, installs and runs modules
without knowing how each emulator works.


ARCHITECTURE
------------

  vcos
    The native runtime: backend selection, input, audio, module loading,
    save states, logging and the guest compatibility shell.

  vcos-module-host
    The isolated process that loads one module at a time. Modules talk to
    the host through a versioned C ABI and never own the display, audio
    device or content loader directly.

  include/vcos_module.h
    The module ABI. It covers module metadata, systems, content, input,
    video, audio, save states and lifecycle.

  modules/
    Built-in implementations. The current modules are a null test module
    and the NES emulator.

  vcos-modman
    The package manager. It validates .vmod archives, checks SHA-256
    hashes, verifies detached Ed25519 signatures and installs modules
    atomically under /var/lib/vcos/modules.

  guest/
    A small 6502 guest shell and demo cartridges used as the compatibility
    path and as a way to exercise the NES runtime.

  buildroot/br2-external/
    The Buildroot configuration that produces the bootable image.


PACKAGES
--------

A .vmod is an uncompressed ustar archive containing:

  manifest.json
  payload/module.so

The manifest declares the module id, version, architecture, ABI version,
entrypoint, supported systems, file extensions and a SHA-256 hash for every
file. Installation checks all of it before writing the module.

A package may be signed. The signature is detached and named after the
package:

  demo-1.0.0.vmod
  demo-1.0.0.vmod.sig

The current signing format is Ed25519 over the package SHA-256 digest. The
public key is embedded in vcos-modman. Unsigned packages are accepted only
with --allow-unsigned and are intended for development.

Installation can use a local file or a direct HTTPS URL:

  vcos-modman install /mnt/demo-1.0.0.vmod
  vcos-modman install https://example.com/modules/demo-1.0.0.vmod

Online installation is direct-URL only. There is no catalog or search yet.


SECURITY MODEL
--------------

The package signature answers one question: did a trusted key sign these
exact bytes?

It does not sandbox the module. Downloaded modules still execute native code
inside vcos-module-host. The current image also uses a development signing
key and a passwordless serial console. Treat this tree as a development
platform until production key handling, seccomp/Landlock isolation and
update/rollback are in place.


BUILD
-----

Host requirements: a C11 compiler, make, Python 3, OpenSSL, curl and
optionally libX11 development headers.

  make test          build and run host tests
  make test-image    boot the Buildroot image in QEMU
  make test-modman-image
                     test local and HTTPS module installation in QEMU
  make run           run the legacy shell in an X11 window
  make run-headless  render the shell headlessly
  make run-systems   build Genesis and open the shell with both systems
  make modular       build the module host and package manager
  make vmod-sign     package and sign the target NES module
  make vmod-genesis-sign
                     build, package and sign the target Genesis module

The full image is produced with:

  ./scripts/get-buildroot.sh
  ./scripts/build-image.sh

Artifacts land in buildroot/output/images/. That directory, the Buildroot
source tree, local signing keys and test output are not stored in Git.

The shell starts with a console menu built from the available content.
Choosing a console opens that console's game list; B returns to the
console menu. NES games run directly in the built-in emulator. Genesis
files (.md, .gen, .smd, .bin) start vcos-module-host with the matching
module, then return to the Genesis game list when it exits. On the
target, Genesis content in /var/lib/vcos/games appears automatically
after the Genesis module is installed. make run-systems demonstrates
the console and game menus on the host with the bundled hello-world ROM.

For the QEMU target, build and sign the Genesis package with
make vmod-genesis-sign, install the resulting .vmod through vcos-modman,
and copy a Genesis ROM into /var/lib/vcos/games.


DATA
----

  /usr/lib/vcos/modules     built-in modules
  /var/lib/vcos/modules     installed modules
  /var/lib/vcos/games       user content
  /var/lib/vcos/saves       save data
  /var/lib/vcos/downloads   temporary online downloads
  /var/lib/vcos/logs        runtime and module logs


ROADMAP
-------

Near term:

  - libretro adapter for reusable emulator cores
  - PlayStation compatibility through a libretro core
  - module sandboxing
  - production signing keys and key rotation
  - update and rollback with save preservation
  - real-hardware validation

Not in scope yet:

  - online catalog or search
  - cloud saves
  - a general-purpose desktop environment
  - distributing BIOS files or commercial games


